; DESERT (GAMEPLAY-DESIGN P1.9). The first biome profile, and the one the user
; asked to see: "show me a large desert map with some dunes, maybe a pyramid,
; ruins, etc. i want to stretch the capability of this engine to trust it to
; carry the identity of the game i feel it should be."
;
; A desert is the HARD case for a vast map and that is why it is first. It has no
; cover to hide the draw distance behind and no buildings to break the horizon,
; so if kilometres of open ground read as interesting here they will read as
; interesting anywhere. It is also P2.5's "open biome" - the one that strips
; cover and makes the approach a plan-your-vector problem.
;
; DUNES ARE LONG AND ROLLING, and they are much BIGGER than the arena's hills
; because the map is much bigger: amplitude 115 m over a wavelength of roughly
; 770 m. That was measured by looking - at 34 m over 450 m the desert read as a
; flat plain from the air, because relief has to be judged against the distance
; you can SEE it from, and this map can see 6 km. ridge 0.12 keeps the swells
; soft; dunes are not ridgelines. Two octaves, because a third puts sand-ripple
; detail on a landform whose whole character is smoothness.
;
; floor_m -8 gives the flat sand between the dunes somewhere to settle, so the
; desert has floors you can fly along rather than being uniformly wavy.

[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="TerrainConfig" load_steps=2 format=3]

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://resources/terrain_config.gd" id="1_tcfg"]

[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_tcfg")
extent_m = 3000.0
cell_m = 16.0
amplitude_m = 300.0
noise_frequency = 0.0006
noise_octaves = 2
ridge = 0.12
floor_m = -120.0
clearing_radius_m = 0.0
clearing_falloff_m = 70.0
